WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:03.180
In this short video, we'll
go through the process

00:00:03.180 --> 00:00:07.470
of deploying a Shiny for
Python application from Posit

00:00:07.470 --> 00:00:08.170
Workbench.

00:00:08.170 --> 00:00:12.540
Here I am in VS Code
to Posit Connect.

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:14.480
So just to give you
a lay of the land.

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:16.140
So what you're
seeing right here is

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:19.410
a Shiny for Python
application called app.py,

00:00:19.410 --> 00:00:21.150
and that resides
in a directory I've

00:00:21.150 --> 00:00:23.850
created called Shiny for Python.

00:00:23.850 --> 00:00:25.380
So to get started,
let's go ahead

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:28.830
and install some packages
we'll need for this workflow.

00:00:28.830 --> 00:00:34.140
So I'm going to use pip to
install the Shiny package.

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:38.430
Also rsconnect-python, which
is used to deploy applications

00:00:38.430 --> 00:00:40.200
to connect.

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:41.820
And then you can
see this application

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:45.180
depends on two popular
data science packages,

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:46.900
matplotlib and numpy.

00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:48.790
So let's make sure we
install those as well.

00:00:52.468 --> 00:00:54.510
I believe I already have
these in my environment.

00:00:54.510 --> 00:00:56.670
So it should say requirement
already satisfied.

00:00:56.670 --> 00:00:57.510
So that's great.

00:00:57.510 --> 00:00:59.330
We are in a good spot.

00:00:59.330 --> 00:01:02.570
Now, before we deploy to posit
connect, let's just make sure

00:01:02.570 --> 00:01:05.750
this application runs
correctly locally

00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:09.050
here within VS Code
running on posit Workbench.

00:01:09.050 --> 00:01:14.350
So I run Shiny run and the
name of the file app.py

00:01:14.350 --> 00:01:14.850
Enter

00:01:18.430 --> 00:01:21.910
And we'll get a URL that
I can then click on OK.

00:01:21.910 --> 00:01:24.780
And here is our Shiny
for Python application.

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:26.920
It's a simple slider
bar that changes

00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:29.110
the number of bins
in the histogram

00:01:29.110 --> 00:01:32.010
as I move it left and right.

00:01:32.010 --> 00:01:35.230
All right. it appears
to be working well.

00:01:35.230 --> 00:01:38.050
And then the last thing we'll
do is go ahead and deploy

00:01:38.050 --> 00:01:41.680
it to Posit Connect and
we'll use the rsconnect

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:43.360
package to help with this.

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:46.990
So rsconnect deploy shiny.

00:01:46.990 --> 00:01:49.300
You have to give it the
name of the Connect server

00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:50.820
you want to deploy to.

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:53.090
So I'll use that -n argument.

00:01:53.090 --> 00:01:57.610
And I have the server saved
In my environment as pct_prod

00:01:57.610 --> 00:01:59.950
and then just give it the
name of the directory that

00:01:59.950 --> 00:02:01.990
houses the app.py file.

00:02:01.990 --> 00:02:03.610
I'm currently within
this directory,

00:02:03.610 --> 00:02:06.920
so I'll just use the period
as a shorthand and hit Enter.

00:02:10.710 --> 00:02:12.180
It'll run through a few checks.

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:14.070
Make sure my
environment is being

00:02:14.070 --> 00:02:16.620
captured and recorded
and sent to connect,

00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:19.820
to replicate that environment.

00:02:19.820 --> 00:02:23.880
And once that is done, it'll
deploy to Posit Connect

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:26.130
and we'll get two URLs
here at the bottom.

00:02:26.130 --> 00:02:28.860
I can click on this bottom one
and it will actually bring me

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:31.140
straight to Posit Connect.

00:02:31.140 --> 00:02:33.210
And here we have that
same application

00:02:33.210 --> 00:02:36.060
running on the Connect
instance, and that

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:39.570
is how you can deploy a
Shiny for Python application

00:02:39.570 --> 00:02:41.990
to Posit Connect.

